- When your work needs something left running (a dev server to look at, a stack to test against), start it with `ws run`, never in the background or with `&`: `ws run -- <command> --port {port}`. This matters: other capsules run the same servers at the same time, and ws gives each run a free port of its own, so yours doesn't collide with theirs on a fixed port, or end up talking to someone else's. The command is given its port as {port} (and $PORT): never pick a port number yourself. ws prints the run's `link`, where it's reached (a name of its own under .localhost when the workspace's router is on, with `local`, the same on 127.0.0.1, beside it): open that, and give it to whoever looks next. A run is ended once your step passes, so leave it running. `ws run ls` lists what's running, and `ws run logs <name>` prints a run's output.
